      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Oak Park Public Library began as a 1903 public library, celebrated for its rich history and early community role.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oak_Park%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1905 the CTA Blue Line extended into Oak Park, originally built on tracks laid by the Chicago Aurora & Elgin interurban.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oak_Park%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The village’s first railroad depot appeared in 1872 on the Chicago & Northwestern Railway, sparking Oak Park’s rise as a Chicago suburb.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oak_Park%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Oak Park’s historic preservation ordinance, first passed in 1972, protects over 2,400 sites, mainly Queen Anne, Prairie School, and Craftsman homes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oak_Park%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Ernest Hemingway’s birthplace and boyhood home, along with Edgar Rice Burroughs’ three residences, are key literary landmarks in Oak Park.",
